\multicolumn{5}{@{}p{5.4in}@{}}{Verdicts are those of the registration, not re-scored here. Deep linear encoder: the closed form gives at $t{=}0$ the count a measurement gives only from about 10\% of $T$ (post hoc trajectory), not ``the closed form beats measurement''; 0.78 of aligned and 0.72 of generic runs have zero outcome, so results on the informative runs are post hoc. E8: $n{=}5$ seeds per row, hardware confounded with row; intervals are percentile bootstrap over seeds, Fieller ($df{=}4$) beside the $f(20)$; the lr share left is the dose reference, not a baseline. Two-layer MLP encoder, gradient descent: scope is the two-layer MLP encoder under GD on this synthetic grid (it does not cover E2's tanh/patch-mask regime); intervals are paired bootstrap over the 72 configurations (each with its 2 seeds); \texttt{r\_decay} is the decaying-$W_1$ criterion, \texttt{r\_init} the E2 predictor with frozen features; the static, $r_{\mathrm{lin}}$ and 0.1\,$T$ rows were fixed before the run but are not registered claims, and the informative-run subsets are chosen from inputs alone. Two-layer MLP encoder, AdamW: AdamW at $\eta{=}0.002$ on this synthetic grid; the static bound is the registered criterion, not a candidate to beat, and the registered reading of a fail is ``no validated AdamW criterion'', not a failure of the delivered theory to transfer; intervals are as in the MLP (GD) encoder (differences are baseline $-$ static, positive means the baseline errs more); \texttt{r\_init}, \texttt{r\_decay} and \texttt{r\_meas} are not defined under AdamW. Two-layer MLP encoder, layer-normalised target: GD at $\eta{=}0.1$ with a layer-normalised target in the MLP (GD) encoder's MLP encoder, on this synthetic grid (AdamW $\times$ LN is not covered); the criterion is \texttt{n2\_decay} because the layer-normalised target test passed and N2 beat \texttt{ln\_static}; the word is ``undetermined'' because the truth is ill-posed (more than 5\% of runs fragile and at most half sample-robust), and the miss is large on every subset taken alone; the first-layer, under-count and rising-rank statements are held-out statements about the premise and about how the criterion fails, not causes, and the conditional row gives no localisation; intervals are as in the MLP (GD) encoder (cluster bootstrap over 40 configurations, three draws each; differences are baseline $-$ \texttt{n2\_decay}); \texttt{mu\_ln\_*} are this study's LN adaptations of the $\mu$ forms; the last row was computed after the score was read. Layer-normalised target: the layer-normalised target under GD only (AdamW $\times$ LN is not covered); intervals are paired bootstrap over the 96 configurations (each with its 5 replicates); the truth is fragile on more than 5\% of runs (all by exactly $\pm1$), so the registered word is the sample-robust one, with the all-run and settled-only verdicts the same; the early-count comparison is read as in the deep linear encoder. E5: ``schedule-affected pairs'' are the (direction, checkpoint) pairs after the first schedule knot at which the truth or a predictor changes alive state; the pair counts in B1a$'$ are for the truth-or-model subset, the paired CIs of the registration are over the larger all-predictor set, and the per-cell bar is applied to cells with at least 100 pairs (the other cell flags in the score file are floor flags, not verdicts); ``stage$_1$'' is the earlier delivered stage model, the other baselines are time-resolved static rules. B1a$'$ fails as registered through its per-cell bar alone. Minibatch noise: verdicts are amendment 1's (written before the held-out run); the first-registered reading is kept in the score file (S3 passes there on the primary scoring; S1 and S4 pass, S2 fails). Level medians below $10^{-13}$ of the top are at round-off. The regrowth indices at $p_0{=}1.5$ (amendment 1 registers an expected 0) are directions that the initial collapse takes below $10^{-13}$ of the top and that grow when their window opens (exact dynamics, the same in 80-bit state), not round-off. Post-loss law: verdicts are amendment 1's, scored once on \texttt{b1p\_fresh} (fresh statistics, values, $p_0$, $\alpha\rho$ values and seeds) and replicated on \texttt{b1p}; cluster CIs resample configurations without their seed; the state agreement differences are in disagreement, so a negative interval means the baseline is closer to the truth than the law; the state agreement CIs are over the larger all-predictor pair set (union overall 0.968), the 0.946 is the truth-or-model subset the bar is applied to; drift is a check \texttt{stage\_qs} shares. E7: counts are target-token eigenvalues $\ge10^{-3}\times$ the largest; the race is the headroom race read at $t_r{=}200$; H2 intervals are bootstrap over the 5 seed clusters (coverage about 83\% at $n{=}5$), with $t$ intervals ($4$ df) beside them, and a verdict needs both to agree; the toggle runs sit on a different GPU family from their reference row; the mean-count comparison for H3 is read from the per-run rows. H1 at epoch 20 replicates the development reading (E3: 0.0070) and is not a prospective test (amendment 2); the prospective tests are H1 at epoch 50, the horizon ratio, and H2 and H3 at epoch 50. Toggle rule (amendment 2): ``holds'' is borne out iff the race error minus the reference row's has absolute value below 2 sd of the reference row's seed-to-seed race error, ``breaks'' iff it is at least 2 sd; a toggle that breaks shows the change is sufficient to break the readout, not that it is the cause. The converged-only reading of the AdamW rank is a secondary scoring and never a pass. The scope test reads ``binding'' only if the unsettled-bin failure exceeds 0.10 and twice the settled one. \texttt{stage\_qs} is the staged model of the schedule account, the baseline of the post-loss law.} \\
